findings.detail:
Nothing writes IVT 0 on kern_big or kern_small outside the BOOT_MARK knob. On the owner's 5150 ROM the dummy masks every IRQ; on any 286+ the div re-runs for ever. Plain input reaches it: ctrl.inc:5966-5975 records a shipped Control Panel gutter click that overflowed a div.

The design:
- A boot-overlay install of vector 0 always, and vector 6 only when cpu_tier >= 286 (never 5, which is PrtSc).
- An ~11-byte resident stub: cli, pop IP and CS, DS=CS, SI = the message, jmp into sch_diepanel.
- The panel takes the message in SI and prints CS:IP where the stack-overflow case prints T_SP, under a generic 'AT' label.
- sched-9's INST_MAX bound (+4) rides with it.

It turns these into a named stop:
- wm-1 and loader-4 (a wild BX into wm_ptr2idx);
- dw-1 (FileSize of 32MB or more);
- gfce-4 (FSX_SPK N=0, with IF=0);
- gfce-5 (deskmod .make);
- every package or C-package divide on file data.

Policy cost: on non-IBM 8088 ROMs a divide overflow currently continues, often harmlessly; it will now stop the machine. That is the kernel's written doctrine (sched.inc:2580-2583).

UNIX, DOS, Win 3.0 and OS/2 all contain a user-code fault to the process that raised it. kern_mega does too, but needs catch frames on every callback (mega_pcin_x).

A real-mode version needs no catches. If the faulting CS is a live instance's segment, or inside a claim it owns, and is not a kernel CS:
- request the instance's close (I_STATE 2);
- toast '<NAME>: divide error';
- set AX=FFFFh;
- IRET.

On an 8088 the pushed IP is already past the div. On a 286+ the div must be skipped: F6/F7 + ModRM + 0-2 displacement bytes + a possible prefix, about 20 bytes. A kernel CS, sch_lock held, or the rate hook in flight still goes to the gfce-1 panel.

The kernel has 103 div/idiv/xlatb sites (95 div, 1 idiv, 7 xlatb; 13 of them in kern_mega-only or knob-only files), and drivers/ has 48 more div/idiv. By reading, the ~90 kernel divide sites outside kern_mega and knob files classify as:
- ~40 with a constant divisor;
- ~20 mouse/pane hit tests, each with a signed guard and an implicit bound (ctrl.inc:5975 was the one that shipped wrong);
- ~20 disk-geometry divides, fenced by the BPB rules and per-call guards;
- 5 pointer-into-table divides;
- 3 on a caller's number.

Five are unfenced, and every one trusts a value that crossed a boundary:
- diskw.inc:3170 and 3932 (dw-1);
- wm.inc:7283 (wm-1);
- deskmod.inc:254 (gfce-5);
- snd.inc:1117 and fsx.inc:4008 (gfce-4; one 8086 site and kern_mega's twin).

The fenced ones are fenced by a comment someone wrote correctly.

FERR, DRVE, LD and FSKE all reuse 1..10 for unrelated meanings, and verb-local codes and bare stc stubs add more. No consumer mixes families, so a renumbered single space buys only one text door and costs every FERR-checking package a rebuild. What is missing is the column a program acts on: DOS 3's class and action. The mapping onto it is total, for example:
- media / user action: NODISK, WPROT, DRVE_DISK, LD_EDISK, FSKE_CHANGED;
- hardware / retry: FERR_IO, DRVE_HW, FSKE_IO;
- not found: FERR_NOENT, DRVE_NOENT, DRVE_NOVOL, FSKE_NOTHERE, FSKE_NOENT;
- out of resource: FULL, DIRFULL, BIG, DRVE_MEM, LD_EBIG, LD_ENOMEM, FSKE_SMALL;
- bad format: DRVE_BAD, LD_EBAD, LD_EVER, FSKE_IMAGE, FSKE_NOFAT;
- locked: FERR_PROT, DRVE_BUSY, DRVE_CLASS;
- app error: FERR_NAME, LD_EABORT, FSKE_ARG;
- internal: DRVE_TWICE.

Cell length is ABI and macros build every cell. In a knob build, OSAPI_SLOT and OSAPI_RCELL emit call/dw apichk_<name> wherever a host-generated build/apichk.inc tags the cell, so every cell keeps its length. The tags are derived from the SDK contract comments:
- W: wm_isrec plus W_SEG == caller DS;
- T: [sch_cur] = 0;
- G: the gfx lock is held, and widths/heights are signed-positive;
- D: DF = 0.

A violation logs to E9 and the debug registry with the cell and the caller's CS:IP, then proceeds ('RIP and continue'). It would have caught wm-1, loader-4, histsys-11, gfx-1 and wm-4 at the first offending call, naming the package.

kbm_isr always far-jumps to the ROM's int 09h, and the real-mode bracket leaves int 09h alone, so the BIOS warm reset answers from inside a hung bracket. video-18's Ctrl+Alt+Esc latch would rescue only a loop that already polls fsx_wait. Two cases defeat Ctrl-Alt-Del:
- an app that replaced int 09h itself;
- a divide fault on a 5150 ROM, which masks IRQ1 (fixed by gfce-1).
